Semantics Elements

Semantics
.provide meaningful tags that clearly describe their purpose
    .clearly define the role and content of elements 
.improve readability, accessibility, and SEO for both humans and browsers

list:
<article> - for content that stands alone and can be independently distributed or reused, e.g blog post/ news article
<aside> - places content in a sidebar, aside from the existing content
    .related to the surrounding content
<details> - defines additional details that the user can hide or view 
<summary> - defines a visible heading for a details element 
<figure>/ <figcaption> - used together in HTML to display an image/ illustration with a descriptive caption
<header> - for the header of a section introductory of a page 
    .can be multiple in a page 
<footer> - at the bottom of any article or document
    .can contain contain details, copyright info
    .can be multiple footers in a page 
<main> - defines the main content of the document
    .should have unique content
<section> - splits page into sections like introduction, contact information, details 
<nav> - defines a set of navigation links in the form of a navigation bar or nav menu
<mark> - highlights the text 

Pointers
.don't overuse <div> - use semantic elements where appropriate instead of non-semantic <div> elements to provide more specific info about the content
.structure content logically - to reflect the meaning and importance of the info
.validate your HTML - use HTML validator to ensure use of semantic elements adheres to HTML5 standards
